    {
      "question": "e-6655",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "f636c9ee46cefcf2bb585fe000162d0f1d857d8b2123aa172f30f5e48ac36205",
      "new_sha256": "917e38159f7afb806c1b2d9bb50831dc2b0e858812a8db4bf8bd4e57cd24d1a9",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-6655",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "First, we decompose the fraction into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4e^{-s}-2}{s} =\\dfrac{4e^{-s}}{s} - \\dfrac{2}{s}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{u}_{1}(t)} & =\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{s},\\,s>0, \\\\ L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1(t)=u(t-1)$ is the unit step function.\n\nTherefore, using the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{4{e}^{-s}}{s}-\\frac{2}{s}} & =4{L}^{-1}{\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{s}}-2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\  & =4{u}_{1}(t)-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\dfrac{4e^{-s}-2}{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{F(s)\\right\\} = f(t)= 4 u_1(t) - 2.\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072336/example-6655.json",
          "file_sha256": "642886ceb7133b8ae825560a48b46bead2572f5a75e9423909cfc25eaa0e97f9"
        }
