        {
          "question": "e-6654",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "From the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{9t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-9},\\,s>9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by applying the inverse Laplace transform to the above result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{9t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-9} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{L{{e}^{9t}}} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-9}} \\\\ {e}^{9t} & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-9}} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-9}} & ={e}^{9t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the multiplicative property of the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\cdot {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-9}}=7\\cdot {e}^{9t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{7}{s-9}}=7{e}^{9t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
