      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-6656",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "First, we write $F(s)$ as a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3s}{(s-4)(s+2)} = \\dfrac{2}{s-4} + \\dfrac{1}{s+2}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4},\\,s>4, \\\\ L{{e}^{-2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+2},\\,s>-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{s-4}+\\frac{1}{s+2}} & =2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-4}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{4t}+{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\dfrac{3s}{(s-4)(s+2)}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{ F(s) \\right\\} = f(t)=2e^{4t} + e^{-2t}.\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072336/example-6656.json",
          "file_sha256": "c35fe783b187e3bab6b95506cb28aed00296ba8dd69bad1a4b153b66552c6276"
